              "end": 1,
              "start": 1
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "Appearances were made by Christina Rainville (Chief Deputy State's Attorney, Bennington, VT.) for the petitioner, Leondra R. Kruger (Assistant to the Solicitor General, Department of Justice) as amicus curiae supporting the petitioner, and William A. Nelson for the respondent."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Justice Alito",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "4b7756ab59de8d030fa5f55d7a49978787d94fe59d9a5b054441f13f20f6ecdb",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-4b7756ab59de-0000-0004"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 12,
              "start": 12
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "Justice Alito asked whether a very important principle was involved that does not simply involve calculating particular periods of delay."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Justice Souter",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "4b7756ab59de8d030fa5f55d7a49978787d94fe59d9a5b054441f13f20f6ecdb",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-4b7756ab59de-0001-0004"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 23,
              "start": 23
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Justice Souter, justice Souter questioned whether the answer would change if the defendant never spoke on the record and only made requests for continuances through counsel."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "4b7756ab59de8d030fa5f55d7a49978787d94fe59d9a5b054441f13f20f6ecdb",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-4b7756ab59de-0002-0000"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 42,
              "start": 42
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "Chief Justice Roberts asked how the argument affects the fundamental principle that public defenders work for their clients, not the State."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "4b7756ab59de8d030fa5f55d7a49978787d94fe59d9a5b054441f13f20f6ecdb",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-4b7756ab59de-0002-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 44,
              "start": 44
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Justice Kennedy, justice Kennedy inquired whether there are instances in which a State\u2011appointed counsel can delay a case without that delay being attributed to the State."
      }
    ],
